  #v(0.25em)
  #set text(size: 9.2pt)
  Sparse linear solvers for elliptic PDEs spend most of their GPU runtime waiting on DRAM. Four-bit weight compression relieved a similar bandwidth limit in neural networks, where small rounding errors average out across wide dense layers. Double-precision Preconditioned Conjugate Gradient (`PCG`) demands host-verified $10^(-8)$ true residuals and a symmetric positive definite (SPD) preconditioner at every iteration, so naive 4-bit rounding can inflate local eigenvalues or break convergence.

  We introduce *BitFSAI*, an extreme-low-bit Factorized Sparse Approximate Inverse framework for `FP64` GPU Algebraic Multigrid (AMG), combining a 4-bit zero-index GPU smoother and guarded `FP8 E5M2` coarse operators with theoretical limits extending down to 1.58-bit ternary, 1-bit sign-only, sub-bit codebooks, and zero-explicit-storage operators (with machine-checked sign-vs-ternary, cut-bound, packing, coarse-bin, and local frozen-word drift theorems). Separating positive `FP32` diagonals and row scales from signed 4-bit off-diagonals preserves $G_q^top G_q succ 0$ unconditionally, while a closed-form $2 times 2$ $A$-energy re-projection in `FP64` setup yields exact unit row energy for the real optimizer across both nonzero and zero off-diagonal directions and accepts the stored `FP32` scalars only when Kahan-verified patch energy satisfies $|E - 1| + 8 epsilon_("mach") sum |t_(a b)| <= 10^(-4)$ (falling back to a verified diagonal row otherwise). Mirroring 4-bit nibbles into the matrix's symmetric CSR pattern eliminates separate factor-index arrays (`ceil(nnz/2) + 8n` bytes in `bitfsai.cuh`; `floor((nnz + n + 1)/2) + 8n` bytes per nonterminal level in `standalone_amg.cu`), and scale-factored Gram application uses `4 B/row` of ordinary smooth payload (with `8(n+1)` bytes of library apply workspace in `bitfsai.cuh` to house fallback `double` scratch and an atomic range flag). For coarse Galerkin operators $A_c$, symmetrized row-sum diagonal repair with grounded-SDDM and normal-range `[2^(-14), 57344]` validation enables unscaled `FP8 E5M2` off-diagonal storage on qualifying coarse levels while falling back cleanly to `FP32` on non-M-matrix or out-of-range levels.

  On an NVIDIA RTX Spark N1X across 8 SPD workloads (up to 7.08M unknowns, $10^4$ contrast, elasticity, and SuiteSparse), our 4-bit GPU implementation cuts persistent smoother factor storage by $2.75 times$–$4.45 times$ (values only) and $4.23 times$–$6.84 times$ (values plus `int32` CSR indices) versus a conventional lower-triangular `FP64` factor on the same `K <= 16` truncated pattern, and by $2.75 times$–$6.45 times$ / $4.45 times$–$9.78 times$ versus a full-pattern lower-triangular `FSAI(1)` factor ($4.84 times$–$12.72 times$ versus mirrored `FP64` values and $1.87 times$–$3.37 times$ versus mirrored `FP16`). Integrated into NVIDIA AmgX with `FP64` outer `PCG` (fixed $omega = 0.72$), BitFSAI passes 8/8 workloads at host `FP64` true relative residual $<= 7.86 times 10^(-9)$ with zero recoveries in timed (`step=2`) trials (while on the separate untimed `step=0` `128³`/`192³` grids, all three `dFFI` smoothers trigger the $r^top z$ guard and recover via `dDDI`). In matched `dFFI` (`FP32` inner V-cycle) precision, `INT4-spl` accelerates pure solve $1.19 times$–$4.20 times$ over matched `dFFI` `JACOBI_L1` on 6/8 workloads (near-parity $1.05 times$ on `128³`, $0.90 times$ on `tet48`), wins paired `resetup + solve` on the two stiffest structural matrices (`2.65`× on `bcsstk14`, `2.68`× on `bcsstk15`; diagonal `JACOBI_L1` wins paired time on the other 6 workloads at `0.37`×–`0.86`×, reflecting BitFSAI's resetup path including CSR validation, per-row Cholesky, and patch-energy checks), and runs at $1.00 times$–$1.12 times$ solve ($0.99 times$–$1.19 times$ paired) versus `dFFI` `BitFSAI FP16`; combining the `dDDI`-to-`dFFI` inner-cycle precision change with `INT4-spl` accelerates pure solve $1.07 times$–$4.38 times$ over stock `dDDI` `JACOBI_L1` across all 8 workloads. In our standalone CUDA-Graph AMG engine on the $192^3$ $10^4$-contrast problem, combining SDDM- and normal-range-guarded `FP8 E5M2` coarse operators (`1.86`× smaller coarse apply-value buffers, `400.6 MiB` to `215.8 MiB`) with sparse-depth polynomial boosting cuts iterations from 28 to 13 and speeds up GPU solve time $1.53 times$ (`1,511.1 ms` to `990.4 ms`; paired $1.03 times$, parity within overlapping 3-trial ranges, with `build_fp8_coarse` performing D2H transfer and host-side connected-component grounding checks during resetup; `poly4@2` with `FP32` $A_c$ reaches $1.48 times$ solve and $1.36 times$ paired from `1,833.3 ms` to `1,347.9 ms`) versus `INT4-spl` + `FP32` $A_c$, while reducing stored smoother-factor plus nonterminal coarse apply-value buffers $1.46 times$ (`589.8 MiB` to `405.0 MiB`, saving `184.82 MiB`) and combined active smoother + coarse-$A_c$ storage $3.51 times$ versus conventional lower-triangular `FP64` (values + indices) + `FP32` $A_c$ (`3.90`× versus mirrored `FP64` + `FP32` $A_c$).

  To establish asymptotic guarantees and fundamental bit-compression limits, we prove a monotone multilevel effectiveness recurrence with both directions of the periodic work-convergence frontier $beta^2 < p$, dimension-, depth-, and contrast-independent spectral condition bounds $kappa_2 (A_0^(1/2) B_0 A_0^(1/2)) <= 17$ (at $t = 2560/1307$) and $<= 9$ (at $t = 30375/15853$) in $O(m+n)$ per-cycle work for certified grounded SDDM hierarchies, an $O((m+n)W^2)$ normalized-kernel finite-precision bit-complexity bound, and sharp sub-4-bit limits: an exact Schur-pivot criterion $D/sigma <= (K-1)^2/(4K)$ and arbitrary-$n$ Global Kaporin Product Identity, a 3-vertex grounded-SDDM separation proving 1-bit sign-only diverges ($kappa ~ 2/t$) where 1.58-bit ternary converges ($kappa -> 1$), a contrast-independent $r+1$ ternary threshold-cut bound for arbitrary $r$-predecessor grounded SDDM patches (matching the $(r+1)/2$ path lower bound in order), projective SPD covering limits down to sub-bit and 0-bit physics-reconstructed operators, and $h/l$ and Kantorovich quadratic $1 + (h-l)^2/(4 h l)$ local frozen-word drift bounds for changing nonlinear tangent operators. All 65 algebraic, Galerkin, operator-effectiveness, scalar-recurrence, bit-limit, and drift theorems are machine-checked in Lean 4 + Mathlib (zero `sorry`, standard axioms only), complemented by 142 Z3 SMT UNSAT obligations (+ 10 SAT controls, spanning exact-real core/multilevel/bit-limit bounds and exhaustive bit-level `BitVec` `INT4`/`FP4`/`FP8` encoding proofs), host C++17 checks, exact-rational/finite-grid audits across 14 grounded SDDM roots, 2 edge-pruned hierarchies, and 63 normalized solves, and clean 4/4 `compute-sanitizer` verification (`memcheck`, `racecheck`, `initcheck`, `synccheck`).
]

// =============================================================================
= Introduction <sec:intro>

Discretizing heat diffusion, linear elasticity, or subsurface flow on a 3D mesh yields a large sparse SPD linear system
$ A x = b, quad A in RR^(n times n), quad A = A^top succ 0. $
Each row couples an unknown to 7 to 75 nearby nodes, while $n$ reaches millions. Production solvers pair Preconditioned Conjugate Gradient (`PCG`) @hestenes1952cg with Algebraic Multigrid (`AMG`) @brandt1986algebraic @ruge1987algebraic @trottenberg2001multigrid. A smoother damps local high-frequency error on each level, coarse Galerkin operators $A_c = P^top A P$ resolve global modes at reduced size, and an outer `FP64` `PCG` loop monitors $r = b - A x$ until $norm(b - A x)_2 / norm(b)_2 <= 10^(-8)$.

On a modern GPU, reading one `FP64` nonzero and its `int32` column index moves 12 bytes to perform two floating-point operations—an arithmetic intensity below `0.17 FLOP/byte`, two orders of magnitude below hardware compute balance. Existing GPU smoothers pay for that gap in different ways. Diagonal smoothers such as `l1-Jacobi` read minimal extra memory yet require hundreds of iterations on anisotropic, high-contrast, or elasticity problems. Incomplete Cholesky (`IC(0)`) @meijerink1977ic @vacek2025mixed relies on sequential triangular solves (`SpTrSV`) that underutilize GPU warps and amplify quantization noise along elimination chains. Conventional lower-triangular Factorized Sparse Approximate Inverse (`FP64 FSAI`) @kolotilina1993fsai @janna2015fsaipack runs completely in parallel and roughly halves iteration counts, yet stores `8.0 B/nnz_L` of factor weights alongside `4` to `8 B/nnz_L` of separate CSR indices (where $op("nnz")_L = (op("nnz")(A) + n)/2$), whereas a mirrored full-CSR `FP64` factor stores `8.0 B/nnz(A)`.

Four-bit and sub-8-bit weight formats (`INT4`, `NF4`, and `FP8`) cut memory traffic dramatically in neural networks @dettmers2022llmint8 @micikevicius2022fp8 @frantar2023gptq @dettmers2023qlora, where rounding errors average across wide dense layers. Inside `FP64` `PCG` targeting host-verified $10^(-8)$ residuals, the preconditioner $M$ must remain symmetric positive definite ($r^top M r > 0$) and spectrally bounded on every iteration: zeroing a single diagonal entry or inflating one local eigenvalue triggers the `PCG` curvature guard ($r^top z <= 0$) and aborts or restarts the solve.

This paper develops *BitFSAI* across both GPU systems and multilevel theory:
1. *Row-level stability and zero-index GPU architecture (Sections 3–4):* We establish four algebraic principles—Gram-SPD invariance, diagonal-split necessity, closed-form $2 times 2$ $A$-energy re-projection (including its zero-direction fallback), and scale-factored Gram application—together with symmetrized row-sum diagonal repair for unscaled `FP8 E5M2` coarse operators, yielding a 4-bit smoother with zero extra CSR indices (`0.5 B/nnz(A) + 8 B/row`) and a CUDA-Graph-capturable multilevel preconditioner.
2. *Live Blackwell GPU evaluation (@sec:eval):* Across 8 SPD workloads on an NVIDIA RTX Spark N1X, we evaluate our standalone CUDA AMG engine (`reference/standalone_amg.cu`), a four-engine comparison against NVIDIA AmgX @naumov2015amgx (in both matched `dDDI` and matched `dFFI` precision), LLNL `hypre` @falgout2002hypre, and Ginkgo `develop` (`2.0.0-dev`) @anzt2022ginkgo, and GPU silicon realizations of SDDM- and normal-range-guarded `FP8 E5M2` coarse operators with sparse-depth polynomial boosting under an explicit per-engine timing and host `FP64` residual verification protocol ($<= 10^(-8)$).
3. *Asymptotic multilevel guarantees and extreme-low-bit limits (@sec:theory):* We prove a three-pass certified damping guard with an arbitrary-dimensional Galerkin Loewner sandwich (and its $2 times 2$ overdamping obstruction), a monotone multilevel effectiveness recurrence (single-level operator step mechanized directly in $A$-inner-product form) with both directions of the periodic work-convergence product-inequality frontier $beta^2 < p$, dimension- and contrast-independent root condition bounds $kappa_2 (A_0^(1/2) B_0 A_0^(1/2)) <= 17$ and $kappa_2 (A_0^(1/2) B_0 A_0^(1/2)) <= 9$ in $O(m+n)$ per-cycle work for certified grounded SDDM hierarchies, a power-of-two-normalized $O((m+n)W^2)$ finite-precision bit-complexity bound alongside state/output lower bounds, and sharp sub-4-bit precision limits (Theorem 9: exact Schur-pivot criterion $D/sigma$, arbitrary-$n$ Global Kaporin Product Identity, 1-bit sign vs. 1.58-bit ternary separation, contrast-independent $r+1$ grounded-SDDM ternary cut bound, sub-bit/0-bit information-theoretic regimes, and frozen-word drift bounds for changing nonlinear tangent operators). All 65 core, multilevel, bit-limit, and drift theorems are machine-checked in Lean 4 + Mathlib @demoura2021lean4 @mathlib2020 alongside 142 Z3 SMT obligations @demoura2008z3 (109 core/multilevel exact-real + 12 exhaustive `BitVec` encoding + 21 bit-limit/drift exact-real obligations). While our theoretical bounds extend down to ternary, sub-bit, and zero-storage operators, the GPU implementation and empirical evaluation in this paper remain focused on 4-bit zero-index smoothers and guarded `FP8 E5M2` coarse operators.

// =============================================================================
= Background and Related Work <sec:background>

Kolotilina and Yeremin @kolotilina1993fsai introduced Factorized Sparse Approximate Inverse (`FSAI`), minimizing $norm(I - G L)_F^2$ (with $A = L L^top$) over lower-triangular matrices $G$ with a prescribed sparsity pattern. Kaporin @kaporin1994new showed that the row-wise optimal factor also minimizes the Kaporin condition number of $G A G^top$. Because every row solves an independent small SPD system over its lower-triangle neighbors, setup and application ($t = G r$, $z = G^top t$) are fully parallel. Adaptive CPU and GPU implementations appear in `FSAIPACK` @janna2015fsaipack, `Chronos` @isotton2021chronos, LLNL `hypre` @falgout2002hypre @yang2002boomeramg, and extended-pattern GPU `FSAI` @laut2024gfsai, all storing lower-triangular factor values in `FP64` or `FP32` with separate factor index arrays.

Mixed-precision sparse preconditioners in Ginkgo @anzt2022ginkgo lowered storage precision to 16 bits. Flegar et al. @flegar2021adaptive stored adaptive block-Jacobi inverses down to `FP16` and `INT16`. Göbel et al. @gobel2021mixed decoupled storage and compute precision for Incomplete Sparse Approximate Inverses (`ISAI`) down to `FP16` while retaining separate index arrays. Tsai, Beams, and Anzt @tsai2023three studied three-precision (`FP64`/`FP32`/`FP16`) GPU AMG in Ginkgo, and Vacek et al. @vacek2025mixed analyzed mixed-precision `IC`-smoothed multigrid building on Meijerink and van der Vorst's `IC(0)` factorization @meijerink1977ic. Production GPU AMG libraries include NVIDIA AmgX @naumov2015amgx (classical `PMIS-D2` and aggregation @notay2010agmg), LLNL `hypre` `BoomerAMG` @falgout2002hypre @yang2002boomeramg (`PMIS` and `ext+i` with `l1-Jacobi` or GPU `FSAI`), Ginkgo @anzt2022ginkgo (`PGM` multigrid with `SpdIsai`), and device-resident blocked GPU AMG with symbolic `RAP` reuse in PETSc @adams2026blocked @adams2026galerkin.

On the multilevel theory side, algebraic multilevel iteration (`AMLI`) @axelsson1989amli, recursive Krylov `K`-cycles @notay2008kcycle, and guaranteed-rate `AMLI` @napov2012amli establish polynomial scheduling for level-independent convergence; approximate Schur complements and linear-size spectral sparsification @durfee2017schur @lee2015sparsification yield near-linear graph-Laplacian coarsening; and McCormick, Benzaken, and Tamstorf @mccormick2021mixed @mccormick2023rounding analyze rounding error in mixed-precision multigrid. Prior GPU sparse smoothers stop at 16-bit storage. BitFSAI reaches 4-bit mirrored factor weights (`0.5 B/nnz(A) + 8 B/row`) with zero duplicate CSR indices, pairs post-quantization $2 times 2$ $A$-energy re-projection with SDDM- and normal-range-guarded row-sum-repaired `FP8 E5M2` coarse operators on live GPU silicon, and provides machine-checked proofs spanning both row stability and asymptotic multilevel convergence.

// =============================================================================
= Smoother Design Principles and Coarse-Grid Precision <sec:principles>

We first establish the four algebraic rules that make 4-bit factorized smoothing numerically sound and bandwidth-efficient, followed by the precision floor for coarse Galerkin operators. All 21 smoother and row-sum repair theorems in this section are machine-checked in Lean 4 + Mathlib (`proofs/lean/Bitfsai/Core.lean`, zero `sorry`, standard axioms only; audit in @sec:audit).

== Gram-SPD Invariance, Diagonal Splitting, and Row Re-Projection

On a 3D $20^3$ anisotropic ($10^(-3), 1, 10^(-2)$), $10^4$-contrast diffusion problem solved by a *two-grid cycle* with an exact `LU` coarse solve (`benchmarks/quant_cliff_and_coarse_floor.py`), 25 power iterations heuristically estimate $lambda_max (M_q A)$ at `1.69` for `FP64` `IC(0)` versus `12.28` at diagonal-inclusive `INT3` (`8.63` at `INT2`). Consequently, undamped `IC(0)` ($omega = 1$) suffers non-SPD two-grid breakdown at `INT3` and `INT2`, and even heuristic spectral damping ($omega = min(1, 1.4 / hat(lambda)_max)$) degrades `IC(0)` from `10` iterations (`FP64`) to `16` (`INT3`) and `25` (`INT2`) (#ref(<fig:principles>)a; note that `IC(0)` is tested with diagonal-inclusive quantization without a split-encoded `IC(0)` control). By contrast, an explicit factorized smoother evaluates $M_q = G_q^top G_q$ directly without triangular inversion, keeping its quadratic form a sum of squares $x^top (G_q^top G_q) x = norm(G_q x)_2^2$, holding $hat(lambda)_max (M_q A)$ near `1.70`, and converging in `14` `PCG` iterations at fixed $omega = 0.72$ down to `INT2`. On this structured 7-point grid where diagonal-to-off-diagonal ratios are bounded, the diagonal-inclusive `INT4` `BitFSAI` control (`INT4inc`) likewise stays stable ($hat(lambda)_max = 1.67$, `13` iterations); as Theorem 2 proves below, diagonal splitting becomes essential once row diagonals dominate off-diagonals on ill-conditioned or stiff matrices.

#thm_box("Theorem 1 (Gram-SPD Invariance, arbitrary n ∈ ℕ)")[
  For any $n in NN$ and any lower-triangular $G in RR^(n times n)$ with strictly positive diagonal entries ($forall i, G_(i i) > 0$), $G$ is injective and $M = G^top G$ is symmetric positive definite (`Matrix.PosDef`), regardless of how the strictly lower off-diagonal entries are quantized. Conversely, if quantization rounds any diagonal entry $G_(k k) = 0$, then $det(G^top G) = 0$ and $G^top G$ fails to be positive definite (`lowerTriangular_posDiag_injective`, `gram_posDef_of_lowerTriangular_posDiag`, `diagonal_split_preserves_posDef`, `lowerTriangular_zero_diag_not_posDef`).
]

Protecting the diagonal is required for conditioning as well as invertibility. Analogous to outlier channels in transformer quantization @dettmers2022llmint8 @dettmers2023qlora, an `FSAI` row diagonal $g_(i i)$ can exceed its off-diagonal couplings by orders of magnitude. When a single row scale spans both $g_(i i)$ and the off-diagonals (diagonal-inclusive `INT4`), small off-diagonal couplings round straight to zero.

#thm_box([Theorem 2 (Spectral Distortion and $2 times 2$ Diagonal-Split Necessity)])[
  Let $A in RR^(n times n)$ be SPD, $G in RR^(n times n)$ be nonsingular, and $G_q = (I + E) G$ with $norm(E u)_2 <= eta norm(u)_2$ for all $u in RR^n$ and $0 <= eta < 1$. For every dimension $n$ and all nonzero $x, y in RR^n$, both the vector-norm sandwich and the pointwise generalized Rayleigh-quotient distortion bound hold:
  $ (1 - eta)^2 norm(u)_2^2 <= norm(u + E u)_2^2 <= (1 + eta)^2 norm(u)_2^2, quad
    (norm(G_q x)_2^2 / (x^top A x)) / (norm(G_q y)_2^2 / (y^top A y)) <= ((1 + eta)/(1 - eta))^2 (norm(G x)_2^2 / (x^top A x)) / (norm(G y)_2^2 / (y^top A y)). $
  Lean mechanizes this pointwise two-vector inequality for every SPD denominator (`conditional_spectral_distortion_bound`, `rayleigh_condition_distortion_bound`); substituting $A arrow.r.bar A^(-1)$ and taking the supremum over $x != 0$ and infimum over $y != 0$ yields the extremal condition-number corollary $kappa_2 (G_q A G_q^top) / kappa_2 (G A G^top) <= ((1 + eta)/(1 - eta))^2$ (strictly below `2.06` at the illustrative perturbation budget $eta = 5/28$, equal to $2.5$ times the symmetric `INT4` half-step $1/14$; `eta_five_over_28_bound`). The distortion factor $((1+eta)/(1-eta))^2$ is attained for every $eta in [0, 1)$ (e.g., $E = op("diag")(eta, -eta)$ on $G = I, A = I$), while at $eta = 1$ singularity ($I + E$ non-invertible) makes the ratio unbounded. Separately, to expose diagonal-inclusive `INT4` collapse on stiff rows, consider the SPD matrix
  $ A_("ex") = mat(1, 1/25; 1/25, 17/10000) succ 0, quad G_("ex") = mat(1, 0; -4, 100), quad G_("ex") A_("ex") G_("ex")^top = I. $
  Diagonal-inclusive symmetric `INT4` (step $100/7$) rounds $-4$ to $0$ because $|-4/(100/7)| < 1/2$, inflating the preconditioned condition number from $kappa_2 = 1$ above `321` ($op("tr") = 18$, $det = 1$). Keeping $g_(i i)$ in `FP32` and scaling only the off-diagonals (diagonal-split `INT4-spl`) encodes $-4$ with code $-7$ and scale $4/7$, recovering $G_("ex") A_("ex") G_("ex")^top = I$ identically (`split_int4_recovers_exact_factor`, `counterexample_2x2_kappa_gt_321`).
]

Even when $M = G_q^top G_q succ 0$, the condition $omega lambda_max (M A) < 2$ is necessary and sufficient for strict positivity of the *symmetrized smoother* $2 M - omega M A M succ 0$, and sufficient for strict positivity of the full V-cycle whenever the coarse correction is PSD. On $A_("ex")$, diagonal-inclusive `INT4` produces $M succ 0$ yet makes $2 M - 0.65 M A_("ex") M$ indefinite (`gram_spd_not_sufficient_for_fixed_damping`). On stiff structural matrices (`bcsstk14`, `bcsstk15` in @sec:four_engine), disabling row re-projection in `dFFI` triggers outer `PCG` curvature-guard (`rz`) failures (recording non-positive $r^top z$ at the outer cycle level without isolating a single level or component), which re-projection in the $A$-norm eliminates.

#thm_box([Theorem 3 (Optimal $2 times 2$ $A$-Energy Re-Projection and Zero-Direction Fallback)])[
  Let $w_("off")$ be the decoded 4-bit off-diagonal vector for row $i$ with patch Cholesky factor $A_("patch") = L L^top$, Gram scalars $c_(11) = norm(L^top w_("off"))_2^2 >= 0$, $c_(12) = (L^top w_("off"))^top (L^top e_m)$, and $c_(22) = A_(i i) > 0$:
  1. *Nonzero off-diagonal branch ($c_(11) > 0$, $Delta = c_(11) c_(22) - c_(12)^2 > 0$):* setting
     $ g_(i i)^* = sqrt(c_(11) / Delta), quad s_i^* = -c_(12) / sqrt(c_(11) Delta) $
     guarantees $g_(i i)^* > 0$ (preserving Theorem 1), exact unit $A$-energy $(G_q A G_q^top)_(i i) = 1$, and global optimality of Kaporin's local ratio $(g^top A_("patch") g) / g_(i i)^2$ over all $(s, g) in RR times RR_(>0)$ (`optimal_2x2_reprojection_properties`).
  2. *Zero off-diagonal fallback branch ($c_(11) = c_(12) = 0$, $c_(22) > 0$):* when all quantized off-diagonals vanish, setting $s_i^* = 0$ and $g_(i i)^* = 1 / sqrt(c_(22))$ likewise guarantees $g_(i i)^* > 0$, exact unit $A$-energy $(g_(i i)^*)^2 c_(22) = 1$, and global Kaporin optimality (`zero_offdiag_reprojection_fallback`). On $A_("ex")$ after inclusive `INT4` zeroes the off-diagonal ($c_(11) = 0$), this diagonal fallback yields $G_("ren") = op("diag")(1, 100/sqrt(17))$ and restores `Matrix.PosDef` of $2 M - 0.72 M A_("ex") M$ (`renormalized_2x2_counterexample_posDef`).
]

To avoid catastrophic cancellation when $c_(12)^2 approx c_(11) c_(22)$, our CUDA implementation (`reference/row_projection.hpp`) computes the component of $v = L^top e_m$ orthogonal to $u = L^top tilde(q)$ in `FP64`, casts $(g_(i i)^*, s_i^*)$ to `FP32`, re-evaluates the stored row's patch energy against the CSR entries with Kahan summation, and accepts only if $|E - 1| + 8 epsilon_("mach") sum |t_(a b)| <= 10^(-4)$ (otherwise replacing the row with the verified diagonal fallback $g_(i i) = A_(i i)^(-1/2)$).

== Zero-Index CSR Sharing and Scale-Factored Application

Compressing factor weights to 4 bits (`0.5 B/nnz(A)`) causes an *index-bandwidth inversion* if separate `int32` column indices are stored and read: streaming separate factor indices in Pass 1 and Pass 2 moves `8.0 B/nnz_L`, dominating weight traffic. Because `FSAI(1)` uses $A$'s lower-triangle sparsity pattern, setup mirrors each quantized 4-bit off-diagonal nibble into both the lower slot $k_("low")$ in row $i$ and the symmetric upper slot $k_("up")$ in row $j$, allocating `0 B` of separate factor indices. Both apply passes stream $A$'s existing `row_offsets` and `col_indices` directly.

#thm_box("Theorem 4 (Scale-Factored Gram Identity, arbitrary n ∈ ℕ)")[
  For any row-scale vector $s in RR^n$ and unscaled factor $tilde(G) in RR^(n times n)$, $G = op("diag")(s) tilde(G)$ satisfies
  $ (G^top G) x = tilde(G)^top (op("diag")(s^2) (tilde(G) x)) quad forall x in RR^n. $
  In the ordinary `FP32` path, Pass 1 writes $y_i = s_i t_i$ (`4 B/row` smooth scratch), eliminating all random `s[c]` global-memory gathers in Pass 2 (`scale_factored_gram_identity`). When extreme matrix scales risk `FP32` underflow or overflow in $s_i t_i$, the apply path disables factoring and restores source scales $s_(c_k)$ in Pass 2 with `double` accumulation.
]

== Precision Tiering and Row-Sum-Repaired `FP8 E5M2` Coarse Operators

Unlike fine-level smoothers, coarse Galerkin operators $A_c = P^top A P$ under $10^4$ coefficient contrast require dynamic range across 8 orders of magnitude and constant-mode preservation. Symmetrizing $Q = Q^top$ and replacing quantized diagonals with
$ tilde(A)_(i i) = A_(i i) + sum_(k != i) (A_(i k) - Q_(i k)) $
guarantees $tilde(A) bold(1) = A bold(1)$ for every dimension $n$, and preserves M-matrix diagonal dominance whenever original row sums are nonnegative and quantized off-diagonals are nonpositive (`rowSum_repair_preserves_ones`, `rowSum_repair_diag_dom`). On the 3D $20^3$ $10^4$-contrast test (#ref(<fig:principles>)b, @tab:coarse), naive `FP16` and `FP8` coarse operators break down in 1–3 iterations, `INT8` with row-sum repair stalls at the 400-iteration limit (whereas naive per-row-scaled `INT8` converges in 155 iterations), and unscaled `FP8 E5M2` (5 exponent bits, normal range $[2^(-14), 57344]$ enforced strictly against saturation and subnormals) with row-sum diagonal repair matches `FP64` in `14` iterations with an `FP64` diagonal (`4.06e-9` vs. `2.55e-9`) and converges in `15` iterations (`6.55e-9`) when persisting the repaired diagonal in guarded `FP32`—requiring zero per-row scale metadata (unlike per-row-scaled `FP16`, `INT8`, and `FP8 E4M3`).

