= GPU Algorithms and Multilevel Cycle Realization <sec:gpu_alg>

Algorithms 1 and 2 detail the CUDA setup and application kernels in `reference/bitfsai.cuh` and `reference/standalone_amg.cu`. We distinguish the exact packing and workspace layouts of the two implementations explicitly.

#alg_box("Algorithm 1: BitFSAI GPU Setup (Zero-Index Packing and Orthogonal Re-Projection)")[
  *Input:* structurally symmetric scalar SPD CSR matrix $A = (mono("rp"), mono("ci"), mono("val"))$ with explicit positive diagonals and split encoding (`INT4-spl` default, or `FP4 E2M1-spl`). Before launching the factor builder, `bitfsai.cuh` validates the `Mode` enum, positive `n`, nonnegative `nnz`, 64-bit allocation and CUDA grid limits, monotonic `rp` (`rp[0]=0`, `rp[n]=nnz`), in-range columns `0 <= ci[k] < n`, and duplicate-free rows, checking `cudaGetLastError()` after every kernel launch and failing cleanly without tainting the CUDA context.
  1. In parallel over rows $i in {0, dots, n-1}$ (one GPU thread per row):
     - Locate $A_(i i) > 0$ and select up to $K$ largest-magnitude strictly lower neighbors ($K <= 15$ for total patch cap $m_max <= 16$ in `bitfsai.cuh`; $K <= 16$ strictly lower neighbors plus diagonal = $17$ patch entries in `standalone_amg.cu`).
     - Assemble $A_(J J)$ and compute its Cholesky factor $L L^top = A_(J J)$ in thread-local `FP64` registers, accepting only finite positive pivots; back-solve $L^top g = e_m$. On factorization failure, fall back to $g = [0, dots, 0, A_(i i)^(-1/2)]$.
     - Set initial scale $s_i = max_(a < m-1) |g_a|$ (or $|g_(i i)|$ if no off-diagonals), quantize $q_a = op("quant")(g_a / s_i)$ into signed 4-bit nibbles (`[-7..+7]` or `E2M1`), and decode $tilde(q)_a = op("decode")(q_a)$.
     - If re-projection is enabled and $m > 1$, form $u = L^top tilde(q)$ and the component of $v = L^top e_m$ orthogonal to $u$ in `FP64`, set $g_(i i)^*$ to its reciprocal norm and $s_i^*$ from the projection coefficient, cast both to `FP32`, and verify the stored row's patch energy error plus $8 epsilon_("mach")$ compensated-sum bound is at most `1e-4`. On zero direction ($c_(11) = 0$) or verification failure, replace the row with the verified diagonal fallback $g_(i i) = A_(i i)^(-1/2)$, $s_i = g_(i i)$, and zero nibbles; reject unrepresentable `FP32` metadata as a setup error.
     - Store `g_diag[i] = (float)g_ii`, `g_scale[i] = (float)s_i`, and mirror each nibble $q_a$ into both lower slot $k_("low")$ and symmetric upper slot $k_("up")$.
  2. Pack nibbles two per byte: `bitfsai.cuh` packs global CSR slots into $ceil(op("nnz")/2) + 8 n$ bytes; `standalone_amg.cu` aligns row starts via $(mono("rp")[i] + i)/2$ into $floor((op("nnz") + n + 1)/2) + 8 n$ bytes per nonterminal level (`0 B` of separate factor indices in both).
  *Workspace accounting:* the ordinary factored `FP32` intermediate payload is `4 B/row` (`float tmp[n]`, e.g., `8.0 MiB` on `128³` fine level, `27.0 MiB` on `192³` fine level). To support runtime `double` fallback on extreme residuals without reallocating, `workspace_bytes<float>()` in `bitfsai.cuh` requests `8(n + 1)` bytes (`4n` ordinary payload plus `double dtmp[n]` overlay and an atomic range flag, managed via an RAII guard if library-allocated), while `standalone_amg.cu` pre-allocates per-level `Work<float>` and `Work<double>` vectors separately (including `poly_rhs`, `poly_acc`, and a per-level atomic `oor` flag captured directly inside the CUDA Graph).
]

#alg_box("Algorithm 2: Scale-Factored Two-Pass Gram Apply and Scale Guard (Theorem 4)")[
  1. *Pass 1 (lower gather):* for each row $i$, accumulate $v_i = sum_(k: c_k < i) op("decode")(q_k) r_(c_k)$ in `FP32` and form $t_i = g_(i i) r_i + s_i v_i$. When the level scale guard passes (`|s_i|/7 in [1e-12, 1e12]` on all rows in `standalone_amg.cu`; `|s_i|/g_ii in [1e-6, 1e6]` and normal `FP32` square-root range in `bitfsai.cuh`), write factored $y_i = s_i t_i$ (`4 B/row`) and atomically flag `oor` if $t_i$ or $y_i$ overflows or underflows to subnormal. Otherwise write unfactored $t_i$ in `double` (multiplying by $omega$ in Pass 1 whenever $0 <= omega <= 1$ to prevent pre-damping `double` overflow in Pass 2).
  2. *Pass 2 (upper gather):* in the factored `FP32` path, skip if `oor` is set; otherwise compute $z_j = g_(j j) (y_j / s_j) + sum_(k: c_k > j) op("decode")(q_k) y_(c_k)$ with zero random `s[c]` reads, using a zero-overhead cold `double` recomputation branch if the pre-damping sum overflows or underflows `FP32` before applying the `double` damping scalar $omega$ (`Smoother::apply` preserves certified `binary64` damping without upward `FP32` rounding). Predicated `double` fallback kernels (captured inside `cudaGraphExec_t` in `standalone_amg.cu`) run only when `oor` is set. When factoring is disabled on a level, both `standalone_amg.cu` and `bitfsai.cuh` promote `t_i` and Pass 2 accumulation to `double`, avoiding extreme-scale `FP32` underflow/overflow.
  3. Apply damped update $x_j arrow.l x_j + omega z_j$. Note that `standalone_amg.cu` uses fixed heuristic damping ($omega = 0.65$ when `max_lower <= 16`, or `<= 3` under `cap3`, and $0.35$ for larger stencils) protected by outer `PCG` curvature ($r^top z > 0$, $p^top A p > 0$) and true-residual guards—distinct from the host certified damping certificate in `reference/certified_damping.hpp` (Theorem 5), which is validated directly on downloaded `BitFSAI INT4_Split` factors in `reference/test_bitfsai.cu`. The terminal dense coarse leaf ($n <= 64$) in `standalone_amg.cu` solves $L y = b,\; L^top x = y$ directly on the Cholesky factor $L$ without forming $A^(-1)$. Outer `FP64` `PCG` checks $r^top z > 0$ and verifies $norm(b - A x)_2 / norm(b)_2 <= 10^(-8)$.
]

== Native `FP8 E5M2` Coarse Operators and Sparse-Depth Polynomial Boosting

Inside `reference/standalone_amg.cu`, we realize two multilevel mechanisms within the captured preconditioner graph (tracking per-level allocation generations so any buffer reallocation across `FP8`/`FP32` mode switches invalidates cached `cudaGraphExec_t` handles automatically):

- *Symmetrized, SDDM- and normal-range-guarded `FP8 E5M2` coarse operators (`fp8coarse`):* On nonterminal coarse levels $j >= 1$ (bypassing the finest matrix $j = 0$ and the dense Cholesky leaf $n <= 64$), numeric setup symmetrizes $A_("sym") = (A_(i k) + A_(k i))/2$, verifies that every nonzero off-diagonal is nonpositive ($<= 0$) and lies strictly inside the normal `FP8 E5M2` range $[2^(-14), 57344]$ (rejecting both saturation $> 57344$ and subnormals $< 2^(-14)$ whose relative rounding error exceeds $12.5\%$), converts off-diagonals to hardware `uint8_t` `__NV_E5M2` (`__nv_cvt_double_to_fp8` with `__NV_SATFINITE`), accumulates exact row sums (including $A_(i i)$) and decoded off-diagonal sums via Neumaier compensated summation in `double`, casts the repaired diagonal to `FP32`, applies a `nextafterf(..., INFINITY)` upward guard preserving both weak diagonal dominance ($>= 0$) and strict positive grounding ($> 0$), and unconditionally verifies that every connected component of the decoded `FP8` graph contains a strictly grounded row. Any level failing the sign, normal-range, or connected-component grounding checks falls back cleanly to `FP32` `af` and frees its `FP8` buffers. Coarse residual kernels decode off-diagonals inline via `__nv_cvt_fp8_to_halfraw`. Note that this GPU `fp8coarse` path uses heuristic damping and unscaled coarse corrections (`gamma = 1`), rather than Theorem 5’s certified smoother and $gamma$-scaled coarse correction.
- *Sparse-depth polynomial boost (`poly<S>[@<O>][:<scale>]`):* Empirically motivated by the sparse-depth polynomial schedule of Theorem 6 (@sec:theory), at selected depths $j equiv O space (op("mod") S)$ (e.g., `poly4@2:1.25`, boosting every fourth level starting at depth 2 with fixed scale $t = 1.25$) the cycle applies two fixed-scale child calls $K_c = 2 t B_c - t^2 B_c A_c B_c$ using pre-allocated level buffers (`poly_rhs`, `poly_acc`; scales $t >= 2.0$ are rejected by default unless explicitly opted in as experimental). Because the degree and scale are fixed, the boosted cycle adds no host synchronization inside the captured `cudaGraphExec_t` preconditioner application (while outer `PCG` dot products still synchronize on the host once per outer iteration).

