= GPU Experimental Evaluation <sec:eval>

== Hardware, Workloads, and Per-Engine Timing Protocol

All GPU experiments execute sequentially (strictly one GPU process at a time) on an *NVIDIA RTX Spark N1X* (6,144-core Blackwell GPU, SM 12.1, Driver `615.78.08`, CUDA 13.4 `V13.4.92`, `aarch64` Linux). Every timed trial runs an untimed `warm` pass (`step = 0`) followed by a `timed` pass (`step = 2`) with seeded right-hand sides (`std::mt19937(12345)`, $cal(N)(0,1)$), gated by an identical independent host CPU `FP64` true Euclidean 2-norm relative residual check $norm(b - A x)_2 / norm(b)_2 <= 10^(-8)$ (NVIDIA AmgX runs with `determinism_flag=0`, so reproducibility is verified via seeded inputs and the $10^(-8)$ residual gate rather than bitwise run-to-run identity). Paired times are computed as the median of within-trial sums (`reset_ms + solve_ms` or `setup_ms + solve_ms`) across $n = 5$ timed repeats ($n = 3$ on `192³`), and we report `[min–max]` trial ranges alongside medians. Because small GPU workloads exhibit `7%–11%` cross-run variance, we treat sub-`1.10×` median differences with overlapping trial intervals as performance parity rather than wall-clock wins. @tab:protocol documents the exact measurement protocol across all four GPU AMG engines.

