We evaluate 8 SPD workloads spanning structured PDEs, finite elements, elasticity, and SuiteSparse @davis2011suitesparse:
- `128³` and `192³` *3D anisotropic high-contrast grids* (2.10M and 7.08M unknowns, 7-point stencil with anisotropy $(10^(-3), 1, 10^(-2))$ and random $4^3$ $10^4$-contrast material blocks refreshed at `step = 2`);
- $P_1$ *Tet* `48³` (111k unknowns, average degree 11.2, conforming 3D tetrahedral FEM diffusion, $10^4$ contrast);
- *Elasticity* `28³` (65.9k unknowns, average degree 72.6, 3D linear elasticity, $nu = 0.42$, $100 times$ stiffness jump);
- *SuiteSparse* matrices `rail_79841_neg` (`Oberwolfach/rail_79841` negated to SPD, 79.8k unknowns, heat transfer), `HB/bcsstk14` (1.8k unknowns, roof stiffness), `HB/bcsstk15` (3.9k unknowns, structural module), and `HB/bcsstk16` (4.9k unknowns, dam structure).

== Standalone CUDA Engine Across 8 SPD Workloads <sec:standalone>

@tab:standalone reports results from `reference/standalone_amg.cu` (`benchmarks/results/standalone_8cases.csv`), comparing `l1-Jacobi`, `BitFSAI FP16`, `BitFSAI INT4-spl`, and `FP64 FSAI` on identical hierarchies and cached symbolic `RAP`. Every format passes 100% of timed trials across all 8 workloads with host `FP64` true relative residual $<= 6.89 times 10^(-9)$ (`max_rel = 6.881e-9`), zero `PCG` recoveries, and at most one diagonal row fallback per timed reset across the entire suite (`1` row per reset on `elastic28`, `0` on all other 7 workloads).

To evaluate memory savings honestly, we distinguish between: (i) a **same-pattern (`K <= 16`) conventional lower-triangular `FP64` factor** storing only the entries BitFSAI actually selects (tracked in `benchmarks/results/pattern_storage.csv` and `summary.json` directly from `standalone_amg --pattern-stats`, since `standalone_amg.cu` caps each row at `MaxLower = 16` lower couplings and zero-pads dropped CSR slots to avoid separate indices—truncating `80.0%` of rows on `elastic28`, `85.5%` on `bcsstk16`, `54.3%` on `bcsstk14`, `41.0%` on `bcsstk15`, and `0%–4.5%` on the four sparse meshes), (ii) a **full-pattern lower-triangular `FSAI(1)` baseline** storing all $op("nnz")_L = (op("nnz")(A) + n)/2$ lower entries ($8 op("nnz")_L$ values, $12 op("nnz")_L + 4(n + L)$ values + `int32` indices), and (iii) `standalone_amg.cu`’s mirrored full-CSR `FP64` / `FP16` representations ($8 op("nnz")(A)$ and $2 op("nnz")(A) + 8 n$ bytes). @tab:standalone reports the full-pattern and mirrored ratios directly from `standalone_8cases.csv`, and the text below gives the exact same-pattern `K <= 16` ratios from `pattern_storage.csv`. Note also that `standalone_amg.cu` executes its captured inner V-cycle in `apply<float>`, casting stored `FP64` factor values to `float` on load; the `FP64` timing column therefore isolates 8-byte factor bandwidth inside an `FP32` cycle rather than `FP64` arithmetic.

