Across all 8 workloads, `BitFSAI INT4-spl` reduces persistent smoother factor storage by **$2.75 times$–$4.45 times$ (values only) and $4.23 times$–$6.84 times$ (values + `int32` indices)** versus a conventional lower-triangular `FP64` factor on the **same `K <= 16` selected pattern** (`elastic28`: `2.76×`/`4.23×`, `bcsstk14`: `4.31×`/`6.62×`, `bcsstk15`: `4.45×`/`6.84×`, `bcsstk16`: `3.45×`/`5.28×`, `128³`: `3.34×`/`5.30×`, `192³`: `3.35×`/`5.31×`, `tet48`: `3.46×`/`5.46×`, `rail_79841_neg`: `2.75×`/`4.45×`), and by **$2.75 times$–$6.45 times$** (values only) and **$4.45 times$–$9.78 times$** (values + indices) versus a full-pattern lower-triangular `FSAI(1)` factor ($4.84 times$–$12.72 times$ vs. mirrored `FP64` and $1.87 times$–$3.37 times$ vs. mirrored `FP16`). On the six workloads where 100% of nonterminal levels pass the $[10^(-12), 10^12]$ scale-factoring guard (`16/16`, `17/17`, `19/19`, `11/11`, `7/7`, `6/6`), `INT4-spl` matches `FP64 FSAI` within 0–2 iterations, delivers clear pure-solve speedups over the `FP64`-stored baseline on five of six ($1.15 times$–$1.57 times$: `128³` `1.57×`, `192³` `1.51×`, `elastic28` `1.28×`, `tet48` `1.23×`, `rail_79841_neg` `1.15×`), and outperforms `FP16` cleanly with non-overlapping ranges on `128³` (`1.12×`, `[415.5–446.8]` vs. `[457.7–497.8]`) and `elastic28` (`1.17×`, `[1049.1–1084.5]` vs. `[1206.3–1248.9]`) while posting faster medians on `tet48` (`1.14×`), `192³` (`1.08×`), and `rail_79841_neg` (`1.06×`; on the tiny 4.9k-row `bcsstk16` matrix, high trial variance yields median `42.93 ms` `[28.08–105.41]` vs. `FP64` `28.48 ms` and `FP16` `31.94 ms`). Compared against standalone `l1-Jacobi` inside this lightweight unsmoothed-aggregation hierarchy, diagonal `l1-Jacobi` is faster in pure solve on 5/8 workloads (`128³`, `192³`, `tet48`, `rail_79841_neg`, `bcsstk16`, medians in @tab:standalone caption), whereas `INT4-spl` beats `l1-Jacobi` on the stiffest structural and elasticity workloads (`elastic28`: `1054.8` vs. `1170.0 ms`; `bcsstk14`: `51.49` vs. `125.54 ms`; `bcsstk15`: `170.77` vs. `206.62 ms`). On `bcsstk14` and `bcsstk15` (entries up to $10^10$), tiny off-diagonal scales trigger the per-level scale guard (`factored_levels` = `5/6` and `3/6`), disabling Theorem 4 factoring on those levels and falling back to unfactored `double` accumulation; combined with 2–6 extra iterations and nibble unpacking on small (`1.8k`–`3.9k` row) matrices, standalone `INT4-spl` runs at `51.49 ms` (`0.89`× vs. `FP64`) and `170.77 ms` (`0.81`× vs. `FP64`, `1.32`× vs. `FP16`).

== Four-Engine GPU Comparison: AmgX, LLNL `hypre`, and Ginkgo `develop` <sec:four_engine>

We evaluate BitFSAI integrated into NVIDIA AmgX (classical `PMIS-D2` V-cycle; public fork #link("https://github.com/bbopen/AMGX")[`github.com/bbopen/AMGX`], branch `bitfsai-smoother`, commit `8f289e59047a000c9a7362aa1564784644c8fdc9`) in *both* matched-precision `dDDI` (`FP64` matrix/vector, `int32` indices) and matched-precision `dFFI` (`FP32` inner V-cycle matrix/vector, `int32` indices, wrapped inside `FP64` outer `PCG`) against stock AmgX `JACOBI_L1` (`dDDI` and `dFFI`), `BitFSAI FP16` (`dDDI` and `dFFI`), LLNL `hypre` CUDA `BoomerAMG` (`PMIS`, `ext+i`, with `l1-Jacobi` and GPU `FSAI`), and Ginkgo `develop` (`2.0.0-dev` @ `796a388`, `Cg<double>` + `Pgm` multigrid with `mg_jacobi_f32`, `mg_isai_f32`, and three-precision `mg_isai_3p`). @fig:four_engine, @tab:four_engine, @tab:paired, and @fig:mem_reproj summarize all measurements from `amgx_8cases.csv` and `prior_art_8cases.csv`.

