Three findings stand out from the four-engine evaluation:
1. *Matched-precision (`dFFI` and `dDDI`) smoother performance and `dFFI` guard sensitivity inside the same hierarchy:* Holding the `PMIS-D2` hierarchy and inner-cycle precision fixed (@tab:paired), in matched `dFFI` precision `BitFSAI INT4-spl` achieves clear pure-solve speedups of **$1.19 times$–$4.20 times$** over matched `dFFI` `JACOBI_L1` on 6/8 workloads (`192³`, `elastic28`, `rail_79841_neg`, `bcsstk14`, `bcsstk15`, `bcsstk16`), near-parity within overlapping ranges on `128³` (`137.5` `[131.2–147.9]` vs. `144.4 ms` `[140.5–163.9]`, `1.05×`), and `0.90×` on `tet48` (`58.9` vs. `52.7 ms`); in paired `dFFI` `resetup + solve`, `INT4-spl` wins clearly on the two stiffest structural matrices (`2.65×` on `bcsstk14`, `2.68×` on `bcsstk15`), whereas diagonal `JACOBI_L1` wins paired time on the other 6 workloads (`0.37×`–`0.86×`: `0.86×` on `rail_79841_neg`, `0.82×` on `elastic28`, `0.77×` on `192³`, `0.76×` on `128³`, `0.50×` on `tet48`, `0.37×` on `bcsstk16`), reflecting `BitFSAI`’s heavier per-resetup path (which includes CSR validation, per-row Cholesky factorization, and Kahan-compensated patch-energy verification; amortized over multiple right-hand sides on fixed operators). Against matched `dFFI` `BitFSAI FP16`, `INT4-spl` achieves clear pure-solve speedups on `128³` (`1.11×`, `[131.2–147.9]` vs. `[148.9–174.8]`), `192³` (`1.12×`, `[461.9–488.7]` vs. `[488.3–635.1]`), and `elastic28` (`1.12×`, `[1141.6–1177.5]` vs. `[1273.3–1306.0]`), and matches `FP16` within `0%–8%` on the remaining 5 workloads (`1.00×`–`1.08×` solve, `0.99×`–`1.19×` paired across all 8) while storing $1.87 times$–$3.37 times$ less smoother memory. In matched `dDDI` precision, `INT4-spl` matches `FP16` within 0–4 iterations and `0.96×`–`1.08×` solve time across all 8 workloads while beating `dDDI` `JACOBI_L1` on 6/8 workloads (`1.12×`–`3.59×`). Combining the `dDDI`-to-`dFFI` cycle precision change with `INT4-spl` accelerates pure solve $1.07 times$–$4.38 times$ over stock `dDDI` `JACOBI_L1` across all 8 workloads ($1.02 times$–$1.68 times$ vs. `dDDI` `FP16`); note that this cross-mode comparison also changes the outer `PCG` driver implementation (`dDDI` invokes AmgX's native `PCG` solver directly, whereas `dFFI` uses `bench_amgx.cu`'s custom `FP64` outer `PCG` loop, `SpMV`, reductions, and stopping logic, which the matched-`dFFI` smoother comparisons above hold fixed). Crucially, while every timed (`step=2`) trial of the reprojected and Jacobi configurations across all 8 workloads ran with zero recoveries, on the separate untimed `warm` (`step=0`) `128³` (5/5) and `192³` (3/3) systems *all three* `dFFI` smoothers (`mix_jacobi`, `mix_fp16_rep`, `mix_int4_rep`) triggered the outer `PCG` $r^top z$ curvature guard and fell back cleanly to `dDDI` (24 warm rows in `amgx_8cases.csv`, e.g., `~540 ms` recovered `dFFI` vs. `~157 ms` plain `dDDI` on `128³` `step=0`), underscoring that mixed-precision `dFFI` inner V-cycles are inherently sensitive to `FP32` hierarchy asymmetry and require the outer $r^top z$ guard and `dDDI` fallback regardless of smoother.
2. *Necessity of Theorem 3 re-projection for the guarded `dFFI` pipeline:* #ref(<fig:mem_reproj>)b isolates Theorem 3 inside matched-precision `dFFI` AmgX on `bcsstk14` and `bcsstk15`. Disabling re-projection in `dFFI` (`mix_int4_norep`) results in `0/5` passed on each matrix because the driver aborts on $r^top z <= 0$: stage 0 (`dFFI`) triggers the $r^top z$ guard at iteration 1, `bench_amgx.cu` sets `recovered=1` to attempt stage-1 `dDDI` recovery with the same unprojected smoother, and stage 1 also aborts on $r^top z$ (total `1–2` iterations, final residuals `6.53` and `25.2`, `pass=0`). Under AmgX’s built-in `dDDI` `PCG` (`int4_norep`), the same unprojected smoother still passes `5/5`, increasing `bcsstk14` from `63` iterations (`20.31 ms`) to `69` iterations (`22.32 ms`) and degrading `bcsstk15` from `138` iterations (`57.97 ms`) to `309` iterations (`125.89 ms`, $2.17 times$ slower). Enabling Theorem 3 re-projection restores `5/5` `dFFI` convergence (`16.61 ms` and `54.24 ms`) with zero recoveries.
3. *Complete solver configurations across libraries:* Comparing full solver configurations across engines, AmgX with `dFFI` BitFSAI (`FP32` inner cycle, `8e-9` inner stop) is the fastest engine in pure solve on 6 of 8 workloads (`128³`, `192³`, `rail_79841_neg`, `bcsstk14`, `bcsstk15`, `bcsstk16`), beating `hypre` `BoomerAMG`’s full-`FP64` best configuration (`7e-9` inner stop) by $1.19 times$–$3.88 times$ (including `1.42×` on `128³` with non-overlapping ranges `[131.2–147.9]` vs. `[184.6–207.9]`, while within AmgX `dFFI` on `128³`, `INT4-spl` `137.5 ms` and `JACOBI_L1` `144.4 ms` are at parity; similarly within AmgX `dFFI` on `bcsstk14`/`bcsstk15`/`bcsstk16`, `INT4-spl` and `BitFSAI FP16` are at parity, with `FP16` posting median `54.03 ms` vs. `INT4-spl` `54.24 ms` on `bcsstk15` under overlapping ranges). Because `hypre` runs its inner V-cycle entirely in `FP64`, those `dFFI` ratios combine precision, hierarchy, outer-loop, and smoother differences; at matched `FP64` inner precision (`dDDI` BitFSAI vs. `FP64` `hypre` best, @tab:paired), `dDDI` BitFSAI still beats `hypre` on `128³` (`156.6` vs. `195.2 ms`), `192³` (`566.6` vs. `715.7 ms`), `rail_79841_neg` (`6.10` vs. `12.2 ms`), `bcsstk14` (`20.31` vs. `64.4 ms`), and `bcsstk15` (`57.97` vs. `65.1 ms`, `1.12×`), while trailing `hypre` on `tet48` (`70.4` vs. `52.2 ms`), `elastic28` (`1451.7` vs. `345.4 ms`), and `bcsstk16` (`20.78` vs. `14.9 ms`). On $P_1$ `Tet 48³`, `hypre` `BoomerAMG` (`PMIS` + `ext+i` + `l1-Jacobi`, `52.2 ms`, `[50.2–52.8]`) and AmgX `dFFI` `JACOBI_L1` (`52.7 ms`, `[51.6–57.0]`) are at parity under our sub-`1.10×` overlapping-range rule, both ahead of `dFFI` BitFSAI (`58.9 ms`). On `Elasticity 28³`, Ginkgo’s `mg_isai_f32` (`Pgm` + `SpdIsai` + `FP32` cycle) achieves the fastest pure solve (`271.5 ms`, `119` iters, ahead of `hypre` `FSAI` at `345.4 ms`, `174` iters, and scalar `PMIS-D2` AmgX with BitFSAI at `1,161.0 ms`, `226` iters), though Ginkgo pays `2,416.7 ms` of cold hierarchy/ISAI setup (