// =============================================================================
= Asymptotic Multilevel Guarantees and Fundamental Limits <sec:theory>

We now establish the formal multilevel convergence guarantees and fundamental precision limits of quantized AMG hierarchies—including variable coefficients, arbitrary weight contrast, nonzero `FP8 E5M2` coarse rounding at every level, and sub-4-bit / frozen-word bounds. All 44 multilevel, bit-limit, and drift theorems in this section are machine-checked in Lean 4 + Mathlib (`proofs/lean/Bitfsai/Multilevel.lean` [28] and `Bitfsai/BitLimits.lean` [16]) and cross-checked by 120 Z3 exact-real UNSAT obligations plus 9 SAT negative controls (`proofs/z3/verify_multilevel_z3.py` and `proofs/z3/verify_bit_limits_z3.py`); the remaining graph-to-recurrence and finite-grid primitive steps of Theorems 7–8 and written network/entropy derivations of Theorem 9 are verified with explicit mechanization boundaries audited in @sec:audit.

== Certified Damping Guard, Galerkin Sandwich, and Overdamping Obstruction

Row-wise unit $A$-energy (Theorem 3) controls $(G_q A G_q^top)_(i i) = 1$, but does not alone bound $lambda_max (G_q A G_q^top) < 2/omega$ for general sparsity patterns (the $3 times 3$ SPD Gram matrix with unit diagonal and off-diagonals `0.99` has $lambda_max = 2.98 > 25/9$). An explicit three-pass entrywise bound certifies both SPDness and, under a Galerkin coarse sandwich, the upper Loewner bound $B_ell prec.eq A_ell^(-1)$.

#thm_box("Theorem 5 (Three-Pass Certified Damping Guard and Galerkin V-Cycle Sandwich)")[
  For any finite SPD matrix $A_ell$, full-column-rank prolongation $P_ell$, and lower-triangular factor $G_ell$ with positive diagonals ($M_ell = G_ell^top G_ell$), define
  $ U_ell = max_i (abs(G_ell) abs(A_ell) abs(G_ell)^top bold(1))_i, quad omega_ell = min(18/25, 1/U_ell), quad R_ell = omega_ell M_ell, $
  computed in three sparse matrix-vector products. Because $lambda_max (G_ell A_ell G_ell^top) <= norm(G_ell A_ell G_ell^top)_infinity <= U_ell$, we have $0 prec R_ell prec.eq A_ell^(-1)$ (with scalar smoothing gain $h <= 1 - (1-h)^2$ for $h in [0, 1]$, `guarded_smoothing_scalar`). Consequently, if the coarse operator $tilde(A)_(ell+1)$ satisfies the explicit Galerkin lower sandwich $gamma_ell (P_ell^top A_ell P_ell) prec.eq tilde(A)_(ell+1)$ ($gamma_ell > 0$, including exact unrounded $tilde(A)_(ell+1) = P_ell^top A_ell P_ell$ at $gamma_ell = 1$) and $B_(ell+1) = B_(ell+1)^top$ satisfies $0 prec.eq B_(ell+1) prec.eq tilde(A)_(ell+1)^(-1)$, then $T_ell = P_ell^top A_ell P_ell$ and $tilde(A)_(ell+1)$ are SPD, $gamma_ell tilde(A)_(ell+1)^(-1) prec.eq T_ell^(-1)$, and the symmetric two-level V-cycle operator
  $ B_ell = 2 R_ell - R_ell A_ell R_ell + gamma_ell (I - R_ell A_ell) P_ell B_(ell+1) P_ell^top (I - A_ell R_ell) $
  satisfies $B_ell^top = B_ell$ and $0 prec R_ell prec.eq B_ell prec.eq A_ell^(-1)$ in arbitrary finite dimensions (`vcycle_upper_bound_of_galerkin_sandwich`). In particular, for SDDM Galerkin operators $A_c = P^top A P$ rounded symmetrically with relative edge error at most $u$ ($u = 1/8$ for normal-range `FP8 E5M2`) and exact row-sum repair, summing the per-edge scalar inequality $(1-u) w_(i j) <= tilde(w)_(i j) <= (1+u) w_(i j)$ (Z3 `e5m2_coarse_sandwich`, which checks the scalar edge bound given the relative rounding premise) over graph edges yields $(1-u) A_c prec.eq tilde(A)_c prec.eq (1+u) A_c$, so applying `vcycle_upper_bound_of_galerkin_sandwich` with $gamma = 1 - u = 7/8$ preserves $B_ell prec.eq A_ell^(-1)$ at every finite depth; the accompanying W-cycle fixed-point ratio $alpha = ((1-u)/(1+u))^2 = 49/81 > 1/2$ and defect-cycle matrix contraction are mechanized in `fp8_e5m2_wcycle_constants` and `defect_cycle_matrix_reduction`. (As noted in @sec:gpu_alg, `standalone_amg.cu`’s GPU `fp8coarse` path uses heuristic damping and unscaled $gamma = 1$ coarse corrections rather than this certified $gamma = 7/8$ scheme.)
]

We implement Theorem 5 in `reference/certified_damping.hpp` using upward-rounded IEEE binary64 arithmetic (`std::nextafter`, checking `FE_TONEAREST`, gradual underflow, symmetry $A = A^top$, and positive diagonals; caller supplies SPDness of $A$), together with a `make_int4_split_factor` / `make_int4_split_factor_from_packed_csr` adapter that certifies real downloaded `BitFSAI INT4_Split` factors (verified directly against GPU apply in `reference/test_bitfsai.cu`) and a downward-rounded `certified_damping_f32` helper (`bitfsai.cuh::apply` accepts `double damp` directly). However, an entrywise absolute-value guard alone can overdamp high-contrast chains: on $A = mat(1, -1; -1, 1 + s)$ ($s > 0$), the exact inverse factor $G = mat(1, 0; s^(-1/2), s^(-1/2))$ satisfies $G A G^top = I$, yet in 1-based indexing $(abs(G) abs(A) abs(G)^top)_(2 2) = (4 + s)/s$ (0-based index `(1, 1)` in Lean's `Fin 2`), forcing $omega <= s/(4 + s) -> 0$ as $s -> 0^+$ (`absolute_guard_overdamping_witness`). Contrast-independent convergence therefore requires tracking upper damping and fine-space smoothing jointly.

== Monotone Effectiveness Recurrence and the Work-Convergence Frontier

#thm_box("Theorem 6 (Monotone One-Level Effectiveness Recurrence and Product-Inequality Frontier)")[
  Let $A$ be SPD, $P$ have full column rank, $T = P^top A P$, $0 prec.eq R prec.eq A^(-1)$ be symmetric, $S = I - R A$, and let symmetric coarse operator $B_("amp")$ satisfy the damped sandwich $alpha g(e) T^(-1) prec.eq gamma B_("amp") prec.eq T^(-1)$ ($gamma > 0$, $alpha, g(e) >= 0$; arising from $l T prec.eq A_c prec.eq h T$ with $gamma = l$, $alpha = l / h$, and a child polynomial correction with energy gain in $[g(e), 1]$). Suppose the weak approximation and smoothing inequality holds in $A^(-1)$ dual form:
  $ S (A^(-1) - P T^(-1) P^top) S^top prec.eq zeta S A^(-1) S^top + K (A^(-1) - S A^(-1) S^top), quad 0 <= zeta < 1, quad K >= 0 $
  (equivalently $S_("sym") Q S_("sym") prec.eq zeta S_("sym")^2 + K (I - S_("sym")^2)$ in $A^(1/2)$-symmetrized coordinates). Then the two-level preconditioner $B_("level") = 2 R - R A R + gamma S P B_("amp") P^top S^top$ is symmetric, satisfies the exact coarse comparison step $A^(-1) - B_("level") prec.eq (1 - c) S A^(-1) S^top + c S (A^(-1) - P T^(-1) P^top) S^top$ with $c = min(alpha g(e), 1/(K + 1 - zeta))$, and obeys the two-sided Loewner effectiveness sandwich $e_("new") A^(-1) prec.eq B_("level") prec.eq A^(-1)$ with
  $ e_("new") = min(p g(e), d), quad p = alpha (1 - zeta), quad d = (1 - zeta)/(K + 1 - zeta) $
  mechanized directly in arbitrary finite dimension without matrix square roots (`monotone_effectiveness_operator`, alongside `monotone_effectiveness_scalar` and `monotone_effectiveness_matrix`). Via weighted Young's inequality ($tau > 0$), the approximation bound follows from a fine-space smoother lower bound $eta Q_star prec.eq H$ and an approximate-lifting defect $norm(Q v)_2 <= norm(Q_star v)_2 + sqrt(epsilon) norm((I - Q_star) v)_2$, giving $zeta = (1 + 1/tau) epsilon$ and $K = (1 + tau)/(2 eta)$. For degree-$m$ Chebyshev acceleration @axelsson1989amli @notay2008kcycle @napov2012amli, $phi_m (e) = tanh^2 (m op("artanh")(sqrt(e))) ~ m^2 e$ as $e -> 0$, and for degree-two Richardson at fixed scale $t$, concavity of $1 - (1 - t x)^2$ bounds the gain on $[u, 1]$ by its smaller endpoint (`two_call_concave_endpoint_min`). Finally, for geometric level contraction $0 < beta < 1$ and retention $0 < p < 1$, a positive periodic degree schedule $m_0, dots, m_(k-1)$ simultaneously satisfies the linear-work product inequality $product_(i=0)^(k-1) (beta m_i) < 1$ and super-unit gain product inequality $product_(i=0)^(k-1) (p m_i^2) > 1$ if and only if $beta^2 < p$: no real positive schedule exists when $p <= beta^2$ (`work_convergence_frontier`, `work_convergence_frontier_periodic`), whereas whenever $beta^2 < p$ there exist integers $k >= 1, m >= 2$ such that $(1, dots, 1, m)$ achieves $beta^k m < 1$ and $p^k m^2 > 1$ (`work_convergence_frontier_converse`).
]

This $beta^2 < p$ characterization governs feasibility of the asymptotic work/gain product inequalities; turning a feasible schedule into uniform multilevel convergence additionally requires the per-level spectral sandwich ($l T prec.eq A_c prec.eq h T$) and smoothing/lifting bound of Theorem 6, which we construct for certified grounded SDDM hierarchies in Theorem 7. Our GPU `poly4` / `poly4@2` experiments (@sec:multilevel_eval) are empirically motivated by this sparse-depth boost mechanism rather than a certified $beta^2 < p$ bound on the heuristic `PMIS`/aggregation coarsener.

== Uniform Four-Phase Quantized Convergence for Grounded SDDM Matrices

#thm_box("Theorem 7 (Uniform Four-Phase Quantized Convergence for Grounded SDDM Matrices)")[
  Let $A_0$ be any SPD grounded SDDM matrix with $n$ vertices and $m$ nonzero off-diagonal edges. At each nonterminal level:
  1. *Linear-time fine-set selection:* conditional expectations of the quadratic potential $Phi(x) = sum_i x_i (1 - sum_(j != i) (-A_(i j)) x_j / (delta d_i))$ select $F$ with internal incident weight at most $delta d_i$ ($delta = 3/4$) and $|F| >= ceil(3 n / 16)$, giving size contraction $beta <= 13/16$. Maintaining each undecided vertex's marginal potential change $Delta_v = 1 - sum_(u != v) (-A_(v u)) x_u / (delta d_v) - sum_(u != v) x_u (-A_(u v)) / (delta d_u)$ and updating only incident neighbors when $x_v arrow.l in {0, 1}$ runs in $O(op("deg")(v))$ per decision, totaling $O(m+n)$ operations (implemented in `select_fine_set_incremental` and verified bit-for-bit identical to the dense $O(n^3)$ oracle across all levels in `verify_sddm_cycle.py`).
  2. *Matrix-free Jacobi lifting:* initialize $F$ with the normalized external coarse/ground weighted mean and run 16 fixed Jacobi sweeps on $L_F = D - Z$, giving lifting defect $epsilon = 7(3/4)^32 < 1/1024$ and $T_star prec.eq P^top A P prec.eq (1 + epsilon) T_star$.
  3. *Sparsified range-extended 3-significand-bit (`FP8 E5M2`-width) coarse operator:* construct a linear-size spectral sparsifier @durfee2017schur @lee2015sparsification within $1 plus.minus 1/128$ of the Schur complement $T_star$ in near-linear randomized setup time (optionally via a 48-term truncated path expansion carrying an extra $63/64$ factor), round each positive edge and ground weight to a range-extended 3-significand-bit floating-point format (E5M2 significand width with relative error at most $u = 1/8$ across arbitrary dyadic exponents, as distinguished from the $[2^(-14), 57344]$ normal range of 1-byte hardware `FP8 E5M2`), and derive diagonals from incident weights. This yields $l = (7/8)(127/128)/(1 + 1/1024)$ and $h = (9/8)(129/128)$ (scaling $l$ by $63/64$ if path-truncated), with coarse correction scale $gamma = l$.
  4. *Certified smoother (two realizations):* either (a) the *protected smoother* $R = (R_B + J_F D^(-1) J_F^top / (1 + delta))/2$ using any upper-certified factor $0 prec.eq R_B prec.eq A^(-1)$ with linear $O(op("nnz") + n)$ application cost ($eta = (1-delta)/(2(1+delta)) = 1/14$), or (b) the *factor-only decoded-block smoother*, partitioning $F$ and $C$ separately into constant-size blocks ($<= 4$), certifying each decoded `INT4` block factor against $(3/4) I prec.eq G_b M_b G_b^top prec.eq (5/4) I$ (bisecting failed blocks down to 4-bit downward-truncated singletons, which always pass since $d_i s^2 in [49/64, 1]$; `singleton_block_truncation_bounds`), and setting $R = (2/5) G^top G$ ($eta = 3/40 > 1/14$).
  5. *Four-phase schedule:* apply one two-call Richardson correction at fixed scale $t$ every four levels (`depth % 4 == 0`), and one call otherwise, ending at an exact $1 times 1$ scalar solve.

  With $tau = 1/8$, combining the single-level operator step of Theorem 6 (`monotone_effectiveness_operator`) across levels with the rational budget certificates (`four_phase_sddm_budgets`, `four_phase_kappa_17_certificate`, `four_phase_kappa_9_certificate`) and Lean’s arbitrary-depth scalar recurrence induction (`four_phase_arbitrary_depth_induction`) yields $zeta = 9/1024$, $K = 63/8$, level cap $d = 145/1297 > 1/9$, common retention $p > 11/15$, and per-cycle work factor $2(13/16)^4 = 28561/32768 < 1$. On the sparsifier success event, each cycle application executes in deterministic $O(m+n)$ arithmetic operations and the symmetric root preconditioner $B_0$ satisfies $e_0 A_0^(-1) prec.eq B_0 prec.eq A_0^(-1)$—bounding the $A_0$-energy spectral condition number $kappa_2 (A_0^(1/2) B_0 A_0^(1/2)) <= 1 / e_0$ independently of dimension, depth, and weight contrast:
  - $kappa_2 (A_0^(1/2) B_0 A_0^(1/2)) <= 17$ at scale $t = 2560/1307$ (calibrating at $u = 27/1280$), and
  - $kappa_2 (A_0^(1/2) B_0 A_0^(1/2)) <= 9$ at retuned scale $t = 30375/15853$ (calibrating at $u = 1331/30375$), with identical $O(m+n)$ cycle-application work.
]

Here $kappa_2 (A_0^(1/2) B_0 A_0^(1/2)) = frac(lambda_max (A_0^(1/2) B_0 A_0^(1/2)), lambda_min (A_0^(1/2) B_0 A_0^(1/2)))$ is the symmetric $A_0$-energy condition number governing `PCG` and energy-norm Richardson convergence (as distinct from the Euclidean singular-value condition number of the nonsymmetric product $B_0 A_0$). Separately, for constant diagonal-tensor 2D/3D diffusion on nested dyadic Freudenthal meshes, conditional on the uniform local Fourier/patch variance, upper eigenvalue ($lambda_max (G A G^top) <= 11/4 < 25/9$), and two-grid approximation bounds ($v^2 <= 1/4$ and 3D `INT4` rounding defect $<= 1/392$), the algebraic deduction formalized in `variance_budget_3d`, `scalar_smoothing_3d` (which takes $lambda <= 11/4$ as a hypothesis), and `vcycle_induction_scalar` shows that the unmodified V-cycle at $omega = 18/25$ contracts uniformly without a coarse sparsifier.

== Finite-Precision Bit Complexity and Information-Theoretic Lower Bounds

Exact-arithmetic condition bounds do not automatically control finite-precision rounding under extreme weight contrast $R_w = w_max / w_min$ @mccormick2021mixed @mccormick2023rounding. Rescaling all level operators by an even power of two $2^(2 s)$ so $a I prec.eq A_i prec.eq I$ (and decoded block factors $G_i$ by $2^s$), normalizing nonzero input vectors by a power of two into $[1/2, 1)$, and pruning negligible coarse weights below $a_i / (512(n_i + 1))$ yields an end-to-end bit-complexity bound alongside information-theoretic lower bounds on self-contained preconditioner state and per-vertex binary output precision.

#thm_box("Theorem 8 (Normalized Finite-Precision Bit Cost and Representation Lower Bounds)")[
  Under the certified grounded SDDM hierarchy of Theorem 7 with root dyadic significand length at most $s_A$, weight contrast $R_w$, and integrated coarse pruning of positive edge and ground weights below $a_i / (512(n_i + 1))$ (retaining factor $511/512$ while preserving $alpha_("pruned")(1 - zeta) > 11/15$, so both $kappa_2 <= 17$ and $kappa_2 <= 9$ remain valid with $gamma_i = l_("pruned")$; `four_phase_pruned_sddm_retention`):
  1. *Normalized arithmetic kernel and single-cycle bit cost:* return $0$ directly when $r = 0$; for $r != 0$, supply a pre-normalized vector $tilde(r) = 2^(-k) r$ with $norm(tilde(r))_infinity in [1/2, 1)$ and separate exponent $k = floor(log_2 norm(r)_infinity) + 1 in ZZ$ (charging input/output exponent representation separately from the normalized kernel). Round $tilde(r)$ onto the shared $2^(-f)$ fixed-point integer grid, evaluate the cycle using the two-pass factored decoded-block smoother ($t_("mid") = G_i v$, $z = (2/5) G_i^top t_("mid")$), matrix-free 16-step Jacobi prolongation and reverse-sweep transpose restriction, exact integer accumulation, and termwise nearest-even rational division, and re-attach $2^k$ on output. On this normalized kernel, every sparse primitive incurs primal/dual energy error at most $mu = 17 n^2 2^(-f)/sqrt(a)$ and the levelwise forward error obeys $E_i <= 14 mu + c_i E_(i+1)$ ($c_i = 4$ on boosted levels, $1$ otherwise; finite-instance constants audited against measured primitive errors in `verify_sddm_cycle.py`). Setting $f = O(log(2n) + log(2 R_w) + log(2/theta))$ achieves the relative error bound $norm(cal(B)(r) - B_0 r)_(A_0) <= theta norm(r)_(A_0^(-1))$ for every nonzero real scale $r in RR^n$, with the normalized arithmetic kernel costing $O((m+n) W^2)$ bit operations per cycle at word length $W = O(s_A + log(2n) + log(2 R_w) + log(2/theta))$ and auxiliary hierarchy storage $O(n[log(2n) + log(2 + log n + log R_w)])$ bits beyond the encoded root $A_0$.
  2. *Outer convergence and binary floating-point output cast:* normalized residual-recomputed Richardson with certified root floor $e_0$ satisfies relative $A_0$-energy norm error $rho_j = norm(x_j - x^*)_(A_0) / norm(x^*)_(A_0) <= (1 - e_0)^j + C 2^(-f)/e_0$, where $C = O(n^2 (14 L + 1) 4^(ceil(L/4)) / sqrt(a))$ is the levelwise single-cycle error constant from part 1. Requiring working grid precision $f$ large enough that $C 2^(-f)/e_0 <= epsilon / 4$, assuming binary floating-point exponents represent the restored $2^k$ scale without overflow or underflow, and casting each restored coordinate to $t >= ceil(log_2 (4 sqrt(K_0) / epsilon))$ significand bits ($K_0 >= kappa_2 (A_0)$, giving relative cast error at most $epsilon / 2$ in the $A_0$-energy norm) guarantees $norm(hat(x) - x^*)_(A_0) <= epsilon norm(x^*)_(A_0)$ within $ceil(log(4/epsilon) / log(1/(1 - e_0)))$ steps (`130` steps for $e_0 = 1/9$ and $epsilon = 2^(-20)$). Attaining a relative Euclidean residual target $tau$ sets $epsilon = tau / sqrt(K_0)$.
  3. *Information-theoretic lower bounds:* (i) any *self-contained* preconditioner family (storing its own operator state without access to $A$) achieving $kappa_2 (A^(1/2) B A^(1/2)) <= K$ across diagonal SPD matrices $A = op("diag")(1, t_2, dots, t_n)$ ($1 <= t_j <= R_w$) requires $Omega((n-1) log log R_w)$ state bits—matching the $log log R_w$ contrast dependence of the auxiliary hierarchy storage in Theorem 8.1 up to the $log(2n)$ graph-index factor; (ii) on a 3-vertex grounded chain with ground weight $g = 2^(-2k)$ ($k >= 3$, $R_w = 2^(2k)$), achieving relative $A$-energy norm error $norm(hat(x) - x^*)_A / norm(x^*)_A <= 1/4$ in ordinary per-vertex binary floating point requires $t >= k = (1/2) log_2 R_w$ significand bits even under exact internal arithmetic, matching the $Omega(log R_w)$ dependence of $t$ in Theorem 8.2.
]

*Proof sketches of the two lower bounds.* For (i), let $q > K^2$, $M = floor(log_q R_w)$, and index the $(M+1)^(n-1)$ diagonal matrices $A(bold(m)) = op("diag")(1, q^(m_2), dots, q^(m_n))$ with $m_j in {0, dots, M}$, each having weight contrast at most $R_w$. Suppose a single SPD operator $B$ certifies $kappa_2 (A(bold(m))^(1/2) B A(bold(m))^(1/2)) <= K$ for two distinct tuples $bold(m) != bold(m)'$ with $|m_j - m_j'| >= 1$. Comparing the Rayleigh quotients $(e_1^top B e_1) / (e_1^top A^(-1) e_1)$ and $(e_j^top B e_j) / (e_j^top A^(-1) e_j)$ across $A(bold(m))$ and $A(bold(m)')$ forces $q^(|m_j - m_j'|) <= K^2 < q$, a contradiction; thus all $(M+1)^(n-1)$ instances require distinct bit representations, taking at least $(n-1) log_2 (floor(log_q R_w) + 1) = Omega((n-1) log log R_w)$ bits. For (ii), take $k >= 3$ and
$ A = mat(2, -1, -1; -1, 1, 0; -1, 0, 1 + g), quad h = 2^(-k), quad g = h^2 = 2^(-2k) quad (R_w = 2^(2k)), $
with right-hand side $b = (-h, h, h^2)^top$ and exact solution $x^* = (1, 1 + h, 1)^top$, so $norm(x^*)_A^2 = 2 h^2$. For any candidate output $hat(x) = x^* + e$ satisfying $norm(e)_A / norm(x^*)_A <= 1/4$, we have $(e_1 - e_2)^2 + (e_1 - e_3)^2 + h^2 e_3^2 <= (1/16)(2 h^2) = h^2 / 8 < (3 h / 8)^2$, which implies $|e_3| < 3/8$, $|e_1 - e_3| < 3 h / 8$, and $|e_2 - e_1| < 3 h / 8$. For every $k >= 3$, $hat(x)_1 = 1 + e_1 > 1 - (3/8)(1 + h) > 1/2$ and $hat(x)_2 - hat(x)_1 = h + (e_2 - e_1) in (5 h / 8, 11 h / 8) subset (0, 2 h)$. Because every ordinary $t$-bit binary float $>= 1/2$ is an integer multiple of $2^(-t)$, having $0 < hat(x)_2 - hat(x)_1 < 2 h = 2^(-(k-1))$ is impossible when $t <= k - 1$, proving $t >= k = (1/2) log_2 R_w$.

== Extreme-Low-Bit Limits: Sign-vs-Ternary Separation, Sub-Bit Bounds, and Frozen-Word Drift <sec:bit_limits>

While our GPU implementation and benchmarks target 4-bit (`INT4-spl`) smoothers, a central theoretical question is how far below four bits BitFSAI's invariants extend—to 2-bit, 1.58-bit ternary (`{-1, 0, +1}`), 1-bit sign-only (`{-1, +1}`), sub-bit shared codebooks, and zero-explicit-coefficient (physics-reconstructed) operators—and how frozen low-bit codes behave when an underlying nonlinear tangent operator drifts across Newton iterations. Writing any SPD row patch in `(predecessors, current vertex)` order as
$ A_("patch") = mat(H, -H v; -v^top H, v^top H v + sigma), quad H succ 0, quad sigma > 0, $
and defining the optimal squared $H$-energy distance to a codeword line $D(q) = min_t norm(v - t q)_H^2$, Theorem 3 re-projection sets $g_(i i)^* = (sigma + D)^(-1/2)$ and $s_i^* = t g_(i i)^*$ with exact local energy inflation $1 + D/sigma$.

#thm_box("Theorem 9 (Sub-4-Bit Precision Limits, Ternary Grounded-SDDM Bound, and Frozen-Word Drift)")[
  1. *Exact setup criterion and Global Kaporin Product Identity:* For any candidate line $q$ with squared distance $D = D(q)$, the fiber energy decomposes as $sigma + norm(v - t q)_H^2 = (sigma + D) + (q^top H q)(t - t^*)^2$ (`projection_line_pythagorean`). With an exact whitened prefix and one quantized row, the normalized Gram matrix has cross-vector norm $rho$ satisfying $rho^2 = D/(sigma + D)$ and condition number $kappa = (1 + rho)/(1 - rho)$, which satisfies $kappa <= K$ ($K >= 1$) *if and only if*
     $ D / sigma <= (K - 1)^2 / (4 K) $
     (`exact_prefix_kappa_threshold`). Across all $n >= 1$ rows on any prescribed lower sparsity pattern, define the Kaporin condition number $K_("ap")(M) = (op("tr")(M) slash n) / det(M)^(1/n)$. Comparing the patch-optimal unit-$A$-energy factor $G_*$ against any Theorem 3 re-projected factor $G_q$ on the same pattern gives equal traces ($op("tr")(G_q A G_q^top) = op("tr")(G_* A G_*^top) = n$) and row-wise squared diagonal ratios $(G_(*, i i) / G_(q, i i))^2 = 1 + D_i / sigma_i$; substituting into the arbitrary-$n$ determinant-ratio identity (`global_kaporin_product_identity`) shows that global Kaporin inflation equals the exact geometric mean of local factors with no independence assumption:
     $ (K_("ap")(G_q A G_q^top)) / (K_("ap")(G_* A G_*^top)) = (product_(i=1)^n (1 + D_i / sigma_i))^(1/n). $
  2. *Sharp 1-bit sign-only vs. 1.58-bit ternary separation and contrast-independent SDDM cut bound:* On the 3-vertex grounded SDDM star ($H = I_2$, $v = (1, t)$, $sigma = t(1-t)$, $0 < t < 1$), even with exact real-valued `(g_ii, s_i)` and optimal Theorem 3 re-projection, dense 1-bit sign-only (`{-1, +1}`) achieves minimum line error $D_s = (1 - t)^2/2$ and satisfies $kappa <= 3$ *if and only if* $t >= 3/5$, diverging as $kappa ~ 2/t$ as $t -> 0$ (at $t = 1/5$, $D_s/sigma = 2$, $kappa = 5 + 2 sqrt(6) > 9$); 1.58-bit ternary (`{-1, 0, +1}`) achieves $D_t = min(t^2, (1 - t)^2/2)$, satisfies $kappa <= 3$ *if and only if* $t <= 1/4 or t >= 3/5$, and converges to $kappa -> 1$ as $t -> 0$ (at $t = 1/5$, $D_t/sigma = 1/4$, $kappa = (3 + sqrt(5))/2 < 3$; `grounded_star_sign_min`, `grounded_star_ternary_min`, `grounded_star_sign_kappa3_iff`, `grounded_star_ternary_kappa3_iff`, `grounded_star_separation_one_fifth`). Moreover, viewing any $r$-predecessor grounded SDDM patch as a resistor network with effective conductance $sigma$ and sorted harmonic voltages $0 = u_0 <= u_1 <= dots <= u_(r+1) = 1$, Kirchhoff's law implies `gap_k * cap_k <= sigma` on all $r + 1$ threshold cuts; pigeonhole (`largest_voltage_gap_exists`, `sddm_ternary_cut_bound`) guarantees a cut $k$ with $op("cap")_k <= (r + 1) sigma$, giving a sorted-prefix ternary word with **$1 + D_("best")/sigma <= r + 1$ independent of edge-weight contrast $R_w$**—matching in order the unit-conductance path lower bound $E / sigma >= (r + 1)/2$ (`unit_path_ternary_lower_bound`). Outside grounded SDDM, for fixed $(H, v)$ with $v$ outside the finite line union of any fixed finite alphabet in $r >= 2$, $D_("best") > 0$ is fixed and $1 + D_("best")/sigma$ diverges as $sigma -> 0$ (more generally, divergence holds iff $D_("best")/sigma -> infinity$; `verify_bit_limits_z3.py` explicitly enumerates $3 times 3$ ternary and two 2-bit arrow alphabets and $4 times 4$ ternary).
  3. *Sub-bit and zero-explicit-coefficient information-theoretic regimes:* Under projective SPD distance $d_P (A, C) = log kappa(C^(-1/2) A C^(-1/2))$, let $N(cal(F), d_P, log K)$ be the minimum number of closed $d_P$-balls of radius $log K$ centered at arbitrary admissible ambient SPD operators covering $cal(F)$. Any fixed-width self-contained public decoder achieving $kappa <= K$ over $cal(F)$ requires at least $ceil(log_2 N(cal(F), d_P, log K))$ bits, with scalar ratio separation at $h > K^2$ (`projective_packing_separation`) and sharp harmonic-mean coarse weight bin relative error bound $<= (r - 1)/(r + 1)$ on $[1, r]$ attained at endpoints $1, r$ (`harmonic_bin_minimax_relerr`, forcing bin ratio $<= 9/7$ for `FP8 E5M2`'s $u = 1/8$). Consequently: (a) arbitrary SPD matrices with bounded entry contrast and degree admit *unbounded* state even in $2 times 2$ via cancellation; (b) for fixed $K > 1$ and growing weight contrast $R_w >= K^2$, bounded-degree grounded SDDM on known support requires $Theta(log_2(1 + (log R_w) / (log K)))$ bits/DOF in the worst case (attained on anchored diagonal / rooted-tree supports; at $R_w = 1$, 0 bits suffice up to global scale), while unknown labeled topology on bounded-degree graphs requires $Theta_K (n log n + n log(1 + log R_w))$ total bits (lower bound: on $n = 2 m$ vertices with unit grounding, the $(2m-1)!! = 2^(Omega(n log n))$ labeled perfect matchings of edge weight $w = R_w >= K^2$ satisfy $kappa(A(M)^(-1/2) A(M') A(M)^(-1/2)) >= 1 + w > K^2$ via test vectors $z = e_u + e_v$ on a mismatched edge and $y = bold(1)$, preventing any ambient SPD center from covering two matchings; on denser graphs, dividing the encoded spectral-sparsifier budget $O_K (n log n + n log(1 + log R_w))$ by `nnz` falls below `1` bit/nnz whenever average degree $op("nnz")/n$ dominates $Theta_K (log n + log(1 + log R_w))$); (c) smooth Hölder scalar elliptic coefficients $log a in C^s$ of bounded radius $H_0$ on a $D$-cube with public mesh and discretization require mesh-independent $O((H_0/delta)^(D/s))$ bits ($o(1)$ bits/DOF as $n -> infinity$, with $e^(2 delta) <= K$ giving $delta = (1/2) log K$); and (d) given public physical side information $Z = z$ (PDE weak form, mesh, boundary conditions, material generator), the covering number applies to the consistent fiber $cal(F)_z = {A in cal(F) : A "consistent with" z}$, so when $cal(F)_z$ fits in one radius-$log K$ ball (or a singleton for exact reconstruction) explicit coefficient storage drops to **0 bits**—shifting cost to generator description length and Roofline regeneration ($Q_1 < Q_0$ and $F_1/P < Q_0/B$), while Theorem 8.3's worst-case $Omega(log R_w)$ per-vertex binary floating-point output barrier survives.
  4. *Local frozen-word drift for changing / nonlinear tangent operators:* When an SPD tangent operator drifts within $l J_0 prec.eq J_1 prec.eq h J_0$ ($0 < l <= h$), keeping a quantized codeword $q$ frozen and recomputing only Theorem 3's two real scalars $(g_(i i)^*, s_i^*)$ degrades the local row optimality ratio by at most $h/l$ (`frozen_word_loewner_drift`). Whenever the frozen codeword was exact at $J_0$, evaluating the old optimal row as a feasible competitor for Theorem 3 re-projection at $J_1$ and bounding its spectral average via the Kantorovich majorant (`kantorovich_pointwise_majorant`, `frozen_word_kantorovich_quadratic_drift`) tightens the local row degradation to the quadratic bound
     $ L_(J_1)(q) <= (h + l)^2 / (4 h l) = 1 + (h - l)^2 / (4 h l) $
     (equal to $1/(1 - epsilon^2)$ for $l = 1 - epsilon, h = 1 + epsilon$).
]

For the GPU implementation and empirical evaluation in this paper, we keep the smoother at 4-bit (`INT4-spl`) with guarded `FP8 E5M2` coarse operators; Theorem 9 establishes the sharp mathematical boundaries for pushing below 4 bits and for reusing frozen quantized codes across nonlinear Newton steps.

== Formal Verification Audit <sec:audit>

